<h2>Wrapping up</h2>
<p>None of this is new. On the Emacs side, as I said before, <code>rx</code> has
shipped for decades, and the Elisp version is more complete than mine.</p>
<p>The idea of describing patterns with a small DSL instead of raw syntax
isn't new either. Plenty of people have tried it, each in their own
way. One project I like a lot in this space is
<a href="https://zod.dev/">Zod</a>, which I wrote about in my <a href="/posts/zod-tutorial">Zod quick
tutorial</a>. It's not a regexp builder: you compose
small schema pieces, and Zod gives you back a parser and a TypeScript
type from the same construction. It follows the same spirit, though:
build big things out of small named pieces you can read.</p>
<p>If you write Elisp and have never tried <code>rx</code>, open <code>*scratch*</code>, type
<code>(rx (+ digit))</code>, and <code>C-x C-e</code> it. If you write JavaScript or
TypeScript, the file above is yours. And if you port it to another
language, send me a link.</p>
